      "stale_hits": [],
      "global_stale_hits": [],
      "answer": "TL;DR: Thalia was not truly corrupted\u2014she was **ensorcelled and manipulated by the Wolf**, who used that control to direct her actions.\n\nThalia\u2019s state is clearly **magical influence, not corruption**. OBSERVED facts show she was \u201c**ensorcelled by the Wolf**\u201d and even \u201censorcelled (unbeknownst to her),\u201d while also explicitly noting \u201c**Thalia does not have an oily sheen to her eyes**.\u201d In contrast, the oily sheen is a consistent marker of corruption in others (e.g., guards, the Wolf himself), so her lack of it matters.\n\nHer behavior lines up with this: she \u201cserves the Wolf\u201d and even \u201cattacks the group after the Wolf's instruction,\u201d but can also be **convinced of the Wolf\u2019s betrayal**, and her \u201cCharmed status ended\u201d followed by \u201crealizes how dire the situation truly is.\u201d That shift indicates her agency returns once the influence breaks.\n\nThe Wolf\u2019s role ties this together: he is a **cultist, manipulator, and deceiver** who \u201corders Thalia to attack\u201d and actively spreads confusion. He uses ensorcellment as a control method rather than relying solely on corruption.\n\nKey Attributes:\n- Thalia (OBSERVED): ensorcelled by the Wolf; no oily sheen; can be convinced; charm ended\n- The Wolf (OBSERVED): cultist; manipulator; issues commands to Thalia; deceptive influence\n- Corruption Marker (OBSERVED): oily sheen in eyes (absent in Thalia)\n\nBottom line: Thalia was **played, not corrupted**, and the Wolf was the one pulling those strings.",
